        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.488,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.108,
            0.886,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "Archimedes did a ‘thought experiment’; he asked himself what happens if the number of sides of the regular polygon gets larger and larger. Working out the details, he arrived at the result $A = \\pi \\mathbf { r } ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.179,
            0.886,
            0.277
          ],
          "content": "Here perhaps is the most visual and easy-to-understand explanation for why the area of a circle of radius r has area ${ \\pi } r ^ { 2 } ,$ This beautiful visual explanation for the area of the circle was first discovered by Nīlakaṇṭha Somayājī (c. 1500) in his commentary on the Āryabhaṭīya:"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.282,
            0.435,
            0.434
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.337,
                0.44,
                0.367,
                0.456
              ],
              "content": "(A)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_28_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.44,
            0.285,
            0.777,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.633,
                0.44,
                0.661,
                0.456
              ],
              "content": "(B)"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.289,
                0.462,
                0.708,
                0.497
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.37: The slices can be rearranged to form a parallelogram-like structure"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_28_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.505,
            0.885,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": "As the slices become smaller and smaller, the arcs in Fig. 6.37B become more and more closer to a line. This makes the figure more and more closer to a parallelogram with"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.569,
            0.729,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": "base = half the circumference (why?) = πr,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.594,
            0.461,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{height} = \\mathrm{radius} r.",
          "image_source": "images/page_28_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.619,
            0.884,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "This gives a way to argue that the area of the circle is equal to the area of a parallelogram!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.662,
            0.868,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": "Area of the circle = Area of the parallelogram = base × height = πr<sup>2</sup>."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.707,
            0.516,
            0.728
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6.10.1 Area of Sector of a Circle"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.736,
            0.61,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": "A sector of a circle is the region bounded by an arc and the two radii containing the endpoints of the arc."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.795,
            0.612,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": "The area within a sector of a circle may be found the same way that we found the length of a circular arc. Examine Fig. 6.39 and Fig. 6.40."
        },
        {
